1
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:05,150
Let's understand how to manage the Google Cloud infrastructure

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:08,329
without ever clicking a button in the console,

3
00:00:08,439 --> 00:00:10,600
we call this the GitOps,

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:13,489
and we're going to use the Google Cloud config controller.

5
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,149
Let's first understand how this entire flow looks like.

6
00:00:17,319 --> 00:00:20,879
So user will going to push a YAML file into the GitHub.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,950
We have the config sync that detects the changes. And it will pull the

8
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:28,899
YAML file from the Git, we have the config connector.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,549
The config connector converts this YAML file into the

10
00:00:32,549 --> 00:00:37,216
corresponding Google Cloud API calls, and then the Google Cloud

11
00:00:37,216 --> 00:00:41,450
received the request. And according to the request, it is going

12
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:44,306
to make the update into the resources.

13
00:00:44,536 --> 00:00:46,497
Now let's just see how we can do that.

14
00:00:46,817 --> 00:00:47,676
Behind the scenes,

15
00:00:47,696 --> 00:00:51,236
I've already provisioned our management hub by running

16
00:00:51,777 --> 00:00:54,256
gcloud and the config controller.

17
00:00:54,376 --> 00:00:57,646
You can see that our config controller is already running.

18
00:00:57,817 --> 00:01:00,247
That is a dedicated hidden GKE cluster.

19
00:01:01,304 --> 00:01:04,352
The command to create this controller is like this.

20
00:01:04,513 --> 00:01:07,893
Now I have already assigned this controller the owner

21
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:09,744
permission at the project level.

22
00:01:10,064 --> 00:01:14,314
This allows our hub to talk to the Google APIs and

23
00:01:14,314 --> 00:01:16,353
create the resource on our behalf.

24
00:01:16,473 --> 00:01:20,603
You can see that we have here root‑sync.yaml.

25
00:01:20,753 --> 00:01:25,814
Now in this root‑sync.yaml, we are providing our GitHub repository,

26
00:01:25,823 --> 00:01:29,850
and I have already generated the token from my GitHub. You can also

27
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:33,291
generate the token from a GitHub by just coming down here,

28
00:01:33,411 --> 00:01:35,481
going to the Settings tab, and in the bottom,

29
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:36,801
go to the Developer settings,

30
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:40,331
and you can generate a new personal access token from here.

31
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:42,611
Now you provide that Git credential,

32
00:01:42,621 --> 00:01:47,041
so you can run this kubectl create secret generic git‑creds

33
00:01:47,041 --> 00:01:50,371
command by providing your username and the token.

34
00:01:50,691 --> 00:01:56,790
Now once you make those changes and apply it using kubectl apply ‑f

35
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:03,699
root‑sync.yaml, this is going to apply all those changes. And now your

36
00:02:03,699 --> 00:02:08,029
config controller is in sync with your GitHub repository.

37
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:11,449
Now, once you have done that, let's go down to your GitHub.

38
00:02:11,899 --> 00:02:15,789
Now into my GitHub repository, I have created a folder config,

39
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:19,949
and under that I have a YAML, bucket YAML that looks

40
00:02:19,949 --> 00:02:24,369
like a standard Kubernetes manifest, but the kind is a storage bucket.

41
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:28,820
Now here you can see that I'm making one change, and I'm saying

42
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:35,570
that the bucket name which I'm now looking for is ‑2. And the

43
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:38,449
moment I'm going to commit these changes,

44
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:42,050
automatically it will start deploying this specific

45
00:02:42,050 --> 00:02:44,470
bucket into our Google Cloud.

46
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:46,589
I say Commit changes.

47
00:02:47,259 --> 00:02:52,619
And if I go down to here in our Google Cloud,

48
00:02:53,210 --> 00:03:01,740
search for Buckets, right now you can see that earlier the bucket was 2025‑1.

49
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:02,919
Let's refresh,

50
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:06,550
and in the next few minutes, you will see that it has

51
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:09,949
deleted this specific bucket and created a new one

52
00:03:09,949 --> 00:03:12,410
because it wants this to be in sync.

53
00:03:14,990 --> 00:03:18,990
Now here you can see that it has created another bucket and deleted the

54
00:03:18,990 --> 00:03:24,229
older one because I have changed that specific YAML file.

55
00:03:24,559 --> 00:03:28,029
So that's how the config controller can help you to do the

56
00:03:28,029 --> 00:03:31,350
GitOps without touching your Google console.
